; Screamer — EW escort (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P4.2, the sixth roster type).
;
; The design in one line: "it never shoots at you, and it is the most expensive
; thing in the sky." Every number below serves that, and the ones that matter
; are the three RANGES read together:
;
;   preferred_range 40 = where it stations itself, at the edge of the fight.
;   jam_range       55 = where its interference starts to bite.
;   jam_full_range  20 = where your FCS is gone entirely.
;
; That ordering is the whole fight. Meeting it at all puts you around 0.43 jam
; (55-40 over 55-20), so the gun director's window is already almost halved and
; the missile lock is nearly twice as slow before you have done anything. Closing
; to kill it walks you into total jam, where the director is silent, the lock
; refuses outright and the flak fuse degrades to contact-only. **Your gear gets
; worse the closer you get to the thing you have to close on** — which is why the
; jam is a gradient and not a bubble (S.q8, the user's call over my binary lean).
;
; damage 0 / fire_rate 0 are NOT an oversight and must not be "fixed": this type
; is invisible to Layer 1 and reports `mode: none` on Layer 3a, exactly like the
; aegis, and everything it does lives in Layer 2 (BALANCE.md). A screamer that
; shot back would be a raider with a gimmick.
;
; hull 30 against a 25-damage bolt is two chip-gun hits — "light; it's tissue
; once reached" (P4.2), and the reason its P4.3 chip-gun row is `+` rather than
; `0`: the iron trigger still kills it, it just has to be aimed by hand. One
; 60-damage missile would also do it, IF you could get a lock, which is the joke
; the `--` missile row is telling.
;
; speed 15 / accel 16 is raider mobility (14/18) with a hair more top end and a
; hair less authority: it holds a station rather than dogfighting, and it must not
; be able to outrun a player who has committed to closing — an EW asset that can
; simply leave has no counterplay at all.
;
; PROVISIONAL, flagged for hands: jam_range and jam_full_range are FEEL numbers.
; They decide how early the feed starts breaking up and how deep you have to press
; before the gun goes manual, and neither is a thing a bench can judge. Fly it and
; move them (overlay: BESTIARY: SCREAMER).
[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="EnemyConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

